  "market_context": "During the Asian session, USDJPY was in a clear downtrend. A descending triangle formed with a flat support near 154.80 and a descending trendline from ~155.678. Price briefly broke the trendline upward but failed to clear strong resistance above.",
  "key_observation": "The upside break of the descending triangle’s trendline was a fake out—price reversed sharply after failing to sustain above resistance, then broke the support level to resume the dominant downtrend.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst identified a descending triangle on the USDJPY 15-minute chart, bounded by a flat support zone (~154.80) and a descending trendline from a high near 155.678.",
    "Step 2: Price broke the upper trendline to the upside, suggesting potential bullish reversal, but the analyst noted that confirmation required a break above strong resistance above the breakout level.",
    "Step 3: The analyst observed that the breakout failed—price quickly reversed and re-entered the pattern, confirming it as a fake out.",
    "Step 4: Price then decisively broke the support level (~154.80), signaling bearish continuation; the analyst highlighted this as the valid trade signal aligned with the dominant downtrend."
  ],
  "outcome": "Short trade entered after support break, confirmed by bearish engulfing candle; stop loss above engulfing candle, 1:2 risk-to-reward target set.",
  "lessons": "Fake outs often occur at key structure levels when momentum is weak; always require price to confirm beyond resistance (or support) before assuming reversal. Break of prior support becomes resistance and offers high-probability short entries in downtrends.",
